---
title: Settings
---

NAHPU separates project records, shared database records, reproducible user
configs, and installation-only preferences. Open a project, open the
Dashboard menu, and select `Settings` to configure the current installation.

## What each setting affects

| Kind | Examples | Transfer behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project information | Name, accession, location, time zone, dates, project field-ID values | Included in project-info and project transfers |
| Shared database records | Taxa and personnel | Stored in the NAHPU database and associated with projects |
| User configs | Controlled lists and export or document presets | Transfer with `Export user configs` and `Import user configs` |
| Installation state | Theme and current main taxon | Remains on the current installation |

Changing a setting while one project is open can therefore affect other
projects on the same installation. Review transferred user configs before
replacing your current configuration.

## Catalogs

### Format

`Format` selects the main taxon used for new specimen forms on this device. A
project can contain more than one taxon group; this setting only controls the
currently active specimen format and its measurement fields.

### Events and sites

Use `Events` to manage the controlled primary-activity list, collection
methods, event-personnel roles, and environmental fields shown on event forms.
Primary activities default to `Collecting`, `Recording`, and `Observing`;
`Other` is not included unless you add it. Environmental forms initially show
ambient temperature, ambient humidity, cloud cover, rainfall, and notes. Like
Geography settings, the other built-in fields can be shown or hidden without
deleting stored values. Use `Sites` to manage controlled vocabularies for site
and habitat types. Keep these settings consistent across field devices by
transferring user configs.

### Specimens

`Specimens` contains capture-field visibility, field IDs, tissue IDs, specimen
sexes, specimen types, treatments, conditions, and taxon-dependent parasite
settings.

The specimen-sex vocabulary is restricted. `Male`, `Female`, and `Unknown`
remain available for every taxon. Use the `+` chips to add `Gynandromorph`,
`Hermaphrodite`, `Female?`, or `Male?` to specimen dropdowns. Optional chips can
be removed only when no specimen record in the database uses that value.

Under `Field ID`, leave `Use project ID` off to use a Cataloger's initials and
personal field number. The Cataloger must have the `Cataloger` role, field
registration enabled, initials, and a current field number.

Turn on `Use project ID` to configure a prefix, current catalog number, suffix,
and auto-increment behavior. NAHPU concatenates the three values directly. For
example, `NAHPU-`, `120`, and `-M` produce `NAHPU-120-M`.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Changing a project-ID prefix or suffix changes how assigned project IDs are
displayed. Confirm the change carefully if those identifiers have already
been exported or written on labels.

{% /aside %}

## Database

- `Taxa` manages taxonomy records available to projects.
- `Personnel` manages people in the database and their specimen-care roles.
- `Replace database` replaces the active SQLite database from a backup.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Make a current backup before using `Replace database`. Replacing the database
is different from merging a project or importing user configs.

{% /aside %}

## Exports

- `Tabular Data` manages reusable record-export presets.
- `Documents` manages document templates and print-layout presets.
- `Template Editor` creates and edits document templates.

These presets are user configs. Export them when another installation must
reproduce the same columns, labels, or layouts.

## Applications

Use `Theme` to select `System`, `Light`, or `Dark`. Theme choice is local to
the installation. `Data usage` reports the space used by the database, files,
and media; it does not delete anything.

## Transferring user configs

`Export user configs` and `Import user configs` are in the project Dashboard
menu, next to `Settings`; they are not inside the Settings page. User-config
transfer can reproduce controlled lists and presets without replacing the
project database. See [Backup and restore](../../curatorial-guideline/backup-restore)
before replacing configs or data.
